This version records the positions of all odd numbers. For every block of `k` consecutive odd positions, choose a start before the first odd and an end after the last odd.

If the first odd is at index `p`, the number of left choices is the gap from the previous odd, or `p + 1` when there is no previous odd. The right choices work in the same way with the next odd. Multiplying these two gaps counts all subarrays for that block.

An empty array is not expected by the platform, but the loop also handles it safely. The original array stays unchanged.

```java
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

class Solution {
    public int numberOfOddSubarrays(int[] nums, int k) {
        List<Integer> oddPositions = new ArrayList<>();

        for (int i = 0; i < nums.length; i++) {
            if (nums[i] % 2 != 0) {
                oddPositions.add(i);
            }
        }

        int answer = 0;
        int oddTotal = oddPositions.size();

        for (int start = 0; start + k <= oddTotal; start++) {
            int firstOdd = oddPositions.get(start);
            int lastOdd = oddPositions.get(start + k - 1);

            int previousOdd = start == 0 ? -1 : oddPositions.get(start - 1);
            int nextOdd = start + k == oddTotal
                    ? nums.length
                    : oddPositions.get(start + k);

            int leftChoices = firstOdd - previousOdd;
            int rightChoices = nextOdd - lastOdd;
            answer += leftChoices * rightChoices;
        }

        return answer;
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n)`.
Space complexity: `O(m)`, where `m` is the number of odd values.

I changed each number into a simple odd or even check. An odd number adds 1 to the running count, and an even number adds 0. If the current prefix has `oddCount` odd numbers, an earlier prefix with `oddCount - k` gives a subarray with exactly `k` odd numbers.

The map stores how often each prefix count has appeared. The initial `freq[0] = 1` is needed for subarrays that begin at index 0.

Dry run for `nums = [1, 1, 2, 1, 1]` and `k = 3`:
- The prefix counts are `1, 2, 2, 3, 4`.
- At count `3`, the needed earlier count is `0`, so one subarray is found.
- At count `4`, the needed earlier count is `1`, so another subarray is found.
- The answer is `2`.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    int numberOfOddSubarrays(vector<int>& nums, int k) {
        unordered_map<int, int> frequency;
        frequency[0] = 1;

        int oddCount = 0;
        int answer = 0;

        for (int value : nums) {
            oddCount += value % 2;

            auto it = frequency.find(oddCount - k);
            if (it != frequency.end()) {
                answer += it->second;
            }

            frequency[oddCount]++;
        }

        return answer;
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(n)`.
Space complexity: `O(n)` for the map.

Only the parity of each value matters here. I keep a prefix count of odd values and use an array to store how many times each prefix count was seen.

For the current prefix count `oddCount`, the previous count must be `oddCount - k`. Every earlier occurrence of that count creates one valid subarray. The array is enough instead of a `HashMap` because the prefix count can only be between `0` and `nums.length`.

The input array is not changed. If `k` is larger than the total number of odd values, no matching prefix exists and the method returns `0`.

